    "title": "SPD advises safety during New Years holiday - NewsBreak",
    "text": "By Michael McLaughlin ,\n1 hours ago\nSPRINGFIELD, Mo. — Hundreds are expected to gather in Springfield tonight for the New Year’s Eve ball drop, with local authorities urging attendees to prioritize safety amid celebrations.\nMissouri Highway Patrol (MSHP) reported last year that there were 264 traffic accidents during the New Year counting period in Missouri, resulting in 118 injuries and one death.\nCris Swaters, a spokesperson for the Springfield Police Department, stated that officers will be out on patrol during the celebrations.\n“Tonight is going to be business as usual for our officers on patrol. We do have squads that are downtown and will be there for the New Year’s Eve pub crawl as well,” she said.\nShe urged partygoers to plan their return home responsibly, either by picking a designated driver or taking a taxi or ride-sharing service like Uber and Lyft.\nAs the city prepares for a busy night, local bars and pubs like Shot Shack are doing the same.\n“We’re part of the pub crawl, so we’re expecting a full house tonight,” Caitlin Oxenreider, co-owner of Shot Shack, noted a boost in sales.\nOxenreider also emphasized the importance of safety during this time.\n“Definitely travel in groups, come with your friends, make sure your phone is charged,” Oxenreider advised.\nIn addition, two organizations are partnering to provide hundreds of free Lyft rides to people across the show-me state.\nCopyright 2026 Nexstar Media, Inc. All rights reserved. This material may not be published, broadcast, rewritten, or redistributed. For the latest news, weather, sports, and streaming video, head to KOLR - OzarksFirst.com.",
